# Hypothesis (final answer)

The first century in which Diversity in Pottery Decoration rises significantly while Diversity in Pottery Form declines simultaneously is the century beginning 1700 BCE (late 18th century BCE / 17th century BCE strictly, 3650-3551 calBP, in the Late Neolithic / LN cultural period). Across this century, Pottery Form Z drops from +0.74 to -1.13 (dZ_form = -1.87, threshold = -0.50) while Pottery Decoration Z rises from -0.48 to +0.97 (dZ_dec = +1.45, threshold = +0.50), a 1.5-2 sigma opposite-trend shift that survives both d=0.5 and d=1.0 thresholds and is corroborated by a 3-century centred Theil-Sen slope of +0.0093 Z/BCE on Form (positive in BCE-axis, i.e. declining forward in time) and -0.0072 Z/BCE on Decoration (negative in BCE-axis, i.e. rising forward in time). This is also the only century in the 36-transition scan where dZ_form <= -0.5 and dZ_dec >= +0.5 hold simultaneously, so it is unambiguously the FIRST such century in the Bronze-Age / Late Neolithic sequence covered by the dataset.

